    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d357">

                <pb facs="0518" n="406" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg406"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/1368</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 30</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-12">November 12, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Legation’s despatch No.
                    3757 of August 20, 1935,<anchor n="32" xml:id="d357-f1"/> and to previous
                    correspondence in regard to the Shanghai Defense Scheme, the Department concurs
                    in the opinion expressed by you that no useful purpose would be served by
                    proceeding further toward obtaining the revision of Part II, Paragraph 4, of the
                    Defense Scheme as suggested by the Department.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William
                            Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
